.env
data/
logs/
# Every resume document, whatever it is called. The old pattern matched only
# `base_resume.*`, so a resume saved under the person's own name was NOT
# ignored — and this repo is public. Match by extension instead; the .py files
# in resume/ are code and stay tracked.
resume/*.doc
resume/*.docx
resume/*.pdf
resume/*.txt
resume/*.md
resume/*.rtf
resume/*.odt
resume/output/

# Derived from the resume, so it carries the person's titles and seniority.
config/search_profile.yaml
submitter/audit_log/
*.xlsx

__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
.pytest_cache/
.DS_Store

# The application form data: legal name, address, phone, work authorisation,
# demographic answers. This repository is PUBLIC, and unlike a resume — which
# a person chooses to send — these are answers to questions employers ask
# once. Ignored BEFORE the file exists, so it can never be added by a
# `git add -A` on the day it is created.
config/applicant.yaml
config/applicant.local.yaml

# Screenshots and logs from the apply runs: they show filled forms, so they
# carry the same details.
apply/runs/

# The 7.2M-company dataset: 1GB, far past GitHub every limit.
companies/companies_sorted.csv
companies/*.csv
companies/raw_sources/
companies/*.tsv
data/*.exit

# Backups of .env. These carry the same keys as .env itself, and one was
# nearly committed by a `git add -A` -- GitHub push protection caught it.
.env.backup*
.env.bak*
.env.*.backup
